/-!
# Coherent modules on an algebraic space

A module on an algebraic space is **coherent** when it is quasi-coherent and coherent as a
sheaf of modules on the small étale ringed site, which is Stacks 07UA, and the coherent modules
form a full subcategory of all modules, which is the category of Stacks 07UC.

This file also contains the reduction of the characterization of Stacks 07UB(1) ⇔ 07UB(2) —
coherence is quasi-coherence together with finite type, over a locally Noetherian algebraic
space — to two explicit hypotheses: a covering of every étale chart by affine charts with
Noetherian ring of sections, and bijectivity of the base change comparison
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionsBaseChange` between the charts of that covering.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsCoherent` is coherence of a module on an algebraic space.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isCoherent` is the corresponding object property and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.CoherentModules` the full subcategory it cuts out.

## Main results

* `CategoryTheory.ObjectProperty.IsClosedUnderFiniteProducts.of_isClosedUnderFiniteCoproducts`:
  in a preadditive category with finite products, a property closed under finite coproducts is
  closed under finite products.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.coherentModules_isClosedUnderFiniteProducts`: on a ringed space the
  coherent sheaves of modules are closed under finite products.  The abelian structure itself
  is `Stacks.SheafOfModules.coherentModulesAbelian`.
* `SheafOfModules.IsCoherentOnSite.of_isZero`: a zero sheaf of modules on a site is coherent.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isCoherent_containsZero`: the coherent modules on an algebraic
  space contain a zero object.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.coherentModules_abelian_of_closure`: the coherent modules on an
  algebraic space form an abelian category as soon as coherence is closed under kernels,
  cokernels and finite coproducts.
* `CategoryTheory.GrothendieckTopology.coversTop_over_subtype`: if every object of a site is
  covered by morphisms factoring through objects with a property `Good`, then the `Good` objects
  of a slice site cover that slice.
* `LinearMap.mem_ker_fintypeLinearCombination`: membership in the kernel of the
  linear-combination map of a finite family is the vanishing of that linear combination.
* `IsNoetherianRing.exists_fin_generating_relations`: over a Noetherian ring the relations among
  a finite family of elements of an arbitrary module are generated by finitely many relations.
  No finiteness of the module is needed: the relations form a submodule of `A ^ n`, which is a
  Noetherian module.
* `Module.Flat.exists_coeffs_of_sum_tmul_eq_zero`: **flat base change of syzygies.**  If the
  relations among `s₁, …, sₙ` in an `A`-module `M` are generated by `g₁, …, g_k`, and `A'` is a
  flat `A`-algebra, then a vanishing expression `∑ tᵢ ⊗ sᵢ` in `A' ⊗[A] M` has its coefficient
  tuple `t` in the `A'`-span of the images of the `g_j`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_generators_relations_of_isCartesian` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isFiniteType_relations_of_isCartesian`: the relations clause of
  coherence on the small étale ringed site, for an arbitrary module cartesian on affine charts
  over a space covered by affine charts with Noetherian rings of sections.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isCoherentOnSite_of_isCartesian` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isCoherent_iff_isQuasicoherent_and_isFiniteType_of_isCartesian`: the
  resulting hypothesis-carrying form of Stacks 07UB(1) ⇔ 07UB(2).

## Implementation notes

Coherence on the site is `SheafOfModules.IsCoherentOnSite`, Stacks 03DL(8), and
quasi-coherence is `SheafOfModules.IsQuasicoherentOnSite`, Stacks 03DL(6); both are stated for
an arbitrary sheaf of rings on an arbitrary site, so `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsCoherent` only
instantiates them at the structure sheaf of the small étale ringed site.  It is *not*
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.IsCoherent`, which is Stacks 01BV for a ringed space.

Being closed under finite products is the one input of Mathlib's
`CategoryTheory.ObjectProperty.instAbelianFullSubcategory` which coherence arguments never
produce directly: an extension of one summand of a biproduct by the other is a coproduct, so
closure under extensions gives the coproduct form.  The bridge between the two forms is the
first result above.  It is a lemma rather than an instance, so that instance search for
closure under finite products is not redirected through finite coproducts everywhere.

The ambient category of modules on the small étale ringed site is abelian only in the module
universe `u` of the schemes, or in universes at least `u + 1` under the two sheafification
hypotheses recorded in `StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.QuasicoherentGrothendieck`.  The
reduction below is therefore stated at `m = u`, where the hypotheses are instances.

`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.coherentModules_abelian_of_closure` carries closure under
isomorphisms, kernels, cokernels and finite coproducts as hypotheses, and so is weaker than
Stacks 07UC.  Closure under kernels and cokernels contains closure of *quasi-coherence* under
kernels and cokernels, which is Stacks 03M1 and is the standing hypothesis of
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.quasicoherentSheaves_abelian_of_closure` as well, and closure of
coherence on the site, which is Stacks 01BY generalized from a ringed space to a ringed site.

### The relations clause of Stacks 07UB, and why it carries hypotheses

The characterization of Stacks 07UB(1) ⇔ 07UB(2) has an immediate forward direction —
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsCoherent` extends both clauses — and its reverse direction is the
statement that the relations among finitely many sections of a quasi-coherent module of finite
type form a module of finite type on the slice site.  The argument is purely local and needs
exactly two inputs about a chart `A` of the covering:

* the ring `𝒪_F(A)` is Noetherian, so that the relations among `n` sections over `A`, being a
  submodule of `𝒪_F(A) ^ n`, are finitely generated.  Note that this needs *no* finiteness of
  the module: it is `A ^ n` which is Noetherian, not the module;
* for a further chart `A' → A` of the covering, `M(A')` is the base change
  `𝒪_F(A') ⊗_{𝒪_F(A)} M(A)`, so that flat base change carries the generating relations over
  `A` onto the relations over `A'`.  Flatness of `𝒪_F(A) → 𝒪_F(A')` is automatic between affine
  étale charts, by `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.flat_chartRestrict_of_isAffine`.

The second input is carried as the hypothesis `hcart`, and it is asked for **only between the
`Good` charts of the covering**, which is all the argument uses: the comparison occurs once,
over the restriction map from a `Good` chart to a `Good` chart.  For an arbitrary morphism of
affine charts that property is Stacks 03DX(2), and as a hypothesis on the module it is
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsCartesianOnAffineCharts`; for a module trivialized on each `Good`
chart it holds outright, with no cohomological input.

The covering itself is carried as the hypotheses `hcov`, `haff` and `hnoeth` for an abstract
predicate `Good` on charts, rather than derived from local Noetherianity of the space.  The
covering of a chart of a locally Noetherian algebraic space by affine charts with Noetherian
rings of sections is `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_covering_through_noetherianAffine`, and
instantiating `Good` with that covering turns the three hypotheses into the locally Noetherian
`Fact` instance of Stacks 07UB.

The flat base change step is `Module.Flat.exists_coeffs_of_sum_tmul_eq_zero`, which is the
equational criterion for the vanishing of `∑ sᵢ ⊗ tᵢ`
(`TensorProduct.vanishesTrivially_of_sum_tmul_eq_zero_of_rTensor_injective`) rather than the
equational criterion for flatness used by the structure-sheaf case: the elements `sᵢ` live in
an arbitrary module, so the coefficients of a relation are the *second* factor of the tensor
product and the injectivity hypothesis is the one flatness of `𝒪_F(A')` supplies.

The statements about the small étale ringed site are phrased throughout in terms of
`(smallEtaleRingedSite B S hS F hF).C` and `.J` rather than
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.SmallEtale` and `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.SmallEtale.topology`.  The two
are definitionally equal but carry *different* `CategoryTheory.Category` instance terms, and a
`Sieve` built from one of them is not accepted where the other is expected; mixing them also
makes `rw` fail, since keyed matching unfolds neither.  Everything is therefore stated with the
ringed site's own projections, as `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isFiniteType_relations_unit`
is, and the chart-level API (`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.chartRing`,
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.chartRestrict`, `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionsBaseChange`) is
applied explicitly, where definitional unfolding does take place.  For the same reason the
commutativity of `𝒪_F(A)` is introduced by `let` at each chart rather than left to instance
search.

